# /etc/crow/engine-fence.conf — the fence's only input besides `tailscale ip`.
# Install it root-owned, mode 0640 (docs/guide/engine-fence.md). Every address
# below is a documentation placeholder: this file is private to the host and
# holds the real ones.
#
# One directive per line; `#` starts a comment; blank lines are ignored. An
# unknown directive, a bad port, a bad address or a port published twice refuses
# the whole conf, and a refused conf changes no rule.
#
# Nothing here is wildcard: a port is listed because the engine publishes it,
# and the fence decides who may reach it. There is no directive for docker0.

# The one inbound interface allowed to reach a published port. Tailscale's host
# interface is the product default; a host whose tailnet is a container has no
# host-side address and uses the port-only lines plus the fail-open unit variant.
interface tailscale0

# A bridge Crow itself created for one engine, named by its
# com.docker.network.bridge.name. `docker0`, Docker's gateway bridge, the
# hypervisor/CNI defaults and `lo` are refused: a RETURN for a shared interface
# fences nothing. The interactive `check` reads the network's Crow label and its
# com.docker.network.bridge.name and records both in the facts file; `apply --boot`
# reads that record instead of asking docker. A Linux interface name is at most 15 characters.
bridge crow-eng-ex

# Published on this host's tailnet address, v4 and v6, resolved at apply time:
# never write the address here. A tailnet-IP publish carries NO source
# exemptions — only loopback and the engine bridges.
publish tailnet 8003

# No destination match at all. Protects a wildcard or unknown-address publish
# during a migration, and what the fence falls back to for one family when
# tailscale reports no address. Stricter than the addressed rule: it drops that
# port number from every non-allowed interface. It takes no consumer.
publish port-only 8030

# A publish on a LAN address. Source exemptions are allowed here only: one
# address per consumer, and every process on the consumer host counts as the
# consumer. A range needs the operator's explicit `confirm-range` token at the
# end of the line, because it widens who may reach the port; its prefix is a
# number from 1 to 32/128 and `/0` is refused. Every consumer has to be an
# address of this publish's own family. A tailnet address (Tailscale's CGNAT
# range or its ULA prefix) is refused here: that is what `publish tailnet` is
# for, and a tailnet publish carries no source exemptions.
publish lan 192.0.2.10 8011 consumer 192.0.2.20

# Deliberately leave IPv6 unfenced. Only for a host that runs a v6 stack but has
# no ip6tables-restore: without this line such a host is REFUSED (exit 19),
# because a v6-reachable publish would be open to every interface while the v4
# rules looked healthy. With it, no v6 rule is written, state.json records
# "v6": "unfenced-by-operator", and `status` warns that the family is unfenced.
# This is a risk the operator takes on purpose; the safe answer is to install
# ip6tables and delete this line.
#ipv6 unfenced